  <section class="journal-copy">
    <p>I work in design, which means my calendar can get very good at filling itself. Diwali vacation gave me a small gap to make something simply because I wanted to. So I made a tiny world of light.</p>
    <p>This is a custom Diwali effects experience built around five familiar forms: <strong>diya, anar, chakri, kandil and kamal</strong>. Each sits on its own little island, with a name, a colour and a moment of movement. The video above is the showcase: the camera travels through the set, then pulls back so the whole arrangement clicks into view.</p>
    <dl class="journal-facts"><div><dt>What it is</dt><dd>A custom Diwali effects tool, with a motion showcase.</dd></div><div><dt>Who made it</dt><dd>Shubham Shinde, design lead and art director in Bengaluru, India.</dd></div><div><dt>AI in the process</dt><dd>I wrote the concept prompt; AI built the tool, and I directed the creative choices.</dd></div><div><dt>Showcase</dt><dd>14-second isometric motion film.</dd></div></dl>
    <h2>Five familiar things, one little system</h2>
    <p>I wanted each object to feel distinct without making the scene feel like five separate posters. The repeated rounded plinths and angled grid hold everything together; colour and light do the character work. A diya gets a warm flare, the chakri gets its own spin, and the kandil gets a softer glow. The showcase gives each effect a moment before revealing the full composition.</p>
    <p>I wrote the concept prompt and AI built the custom tool from it. My part was giving it direction: deciding which Diwali objects belonged in the experience, what each one should feel like, and how to present its effects in the showcase. I like using AI to get a personalised idea into working form, then making the creative calls that give the result a point of view.</p>
    <h2>A small vacation project, finished enough to share</h2>
    <p>It’s not a campaign pitch or a giant launch. It’s a personal Diwali tool and visual showcase, made during a rare pause in a busy work year—and a nice reminder that even a small tool can hold a whole visual world inside it.</p>
    <p>If you work in <strong>AI-assisted design, motion design or creative technology</strong>, this is the kind of small experiment I like making: take a familiar idea, find a visual rule for it, and see how much personality that rule can carry.</p>
